    shortDescription: "Explain SQL window functions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA window function calculates a value across related rows while retaining the individual rows, using an `OVER` clause.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you understand database behavior under real data volume and concurrency. A strong answer separates logical correctness from query or storage performance.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the database concept or guarantee.\n2. Explain how the engine or schema uses it.\n3. Connect it to query plans, transactions, or data distribution where relevant.\n4. Mention the trade-off or failure case.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the database rule first, then explain what happens to the data or query plan. I would add a concrete SQL or transaction example and mention what changes when the data size or concurrency grows.\n\n**Example :**\n`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY created_at DESC)` can number each customer’s orders without collapsing them into one row.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nWindow functions are useful for ranking, running totals, lag/lead comparisons, and per-group analytics.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming an index or database feature is always beneficial.\n- Ignoring NULLs, duplicates, transactions, isolation, or query plans where they matter.\n- Giving a correctness claim without stating the database behavior that supports it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
